IMPORT prelude

-- Coroners Act 2010 (Singapore) -- reportable deaths and the duty to report them (ss 5, 6, Second Schedule), when an inquiry must be
-- held (s 25, Third Schedule), and what an inquiry may decide (s 27).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/CA2010.txt, with amendments to
-- Act 16 of 2026 (in force 30 September 2026) and S 255/2023 shown.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Cited by 13 of the 527 Singapore Acts deposited in this repository. SCOPED to reporting, the duty to inquire and the
-- purpose of an inquiry. Not encoded: appointments, police investigation powers, post-mortems and release of bodies, inquiry
-- procedure, deaths outside Singapore (s 24(1)(e), (f)), and the Public Prosecutor's power to require an inquiry beyond its effect.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Coroners Act 2010 -- deaths`

DECLARE Death IS ONE OF
    `an elderly person dying of illness at home under a doctor's care`
    `an unidentified body`
    `a suicide`
    `a road accident`
    `a death after surgery`
    `a death in prison custody`
    `a death after release related to custody`
    `a judicial execution`
    `a death during a police operation`
    `a workplace accident`
    `a death on an MRT train`
    `a suspected homicide`
    `a death of unknown cause`

§§ `5, 6 and Second Schedule -- reportable deaths`

-- Second Schedule: reportable deaths include (1) unidentified; (2) unnatural or violent; (3) accidents; (4) "as a result of any medical
-- treatment or care"; (5) in official custody, "except death as a result of the lawful execution of a death sentence"; (6) after
-- custody and related to it; (7) law enforcement operations; (8) workplace accidents; (9) public transport; (10) Singapore ships and
-- aircraft in flight; (11) unlawful act or omission; (12) unknown cause; (13) suspicious circumstances.
-- s 5(1): ANY person who becomes aware of an apparently reportable death must report it to a police officer as soon as reasonably
-- practicable; (2) without reasonable excuse -- "the burden of proving which is on the accused" -- $1,500 or one month.
-- s 6(1): a death in arrest or custody must be reported by the custodian within 24 HOURS; (2) not for a lawful execution; (3) $10,000
-- or 6 months.
GIVEN `the death` IS A Death
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the death is reportable` IF
    CONSIDER `the death`
    WHEN `an elderly person dying of illness at home under a doctor's care` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a judicial execution` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `knew of an apparently reportable death` IS A BOOLEAN, `reported to police` IS A BOOLEAN, `proved a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `failing to report is an offence` IF
    `knew of an apparently reportable death` AND (NOT `reported to police`) AND (NOT `proved a reasonable excuse`)

§§ `25 and Third Schedule -- when an inquiry must be held`

-- s 25(1): a Coroner MUST hold an inquiry where (a) the person died in official custody; (b) the death was a lawful execution; (c) the
-- Public Prosecutor requires it; or (d) the death falls in the Third Schedule "unless the Coroner is satisfied that the death was due
-- to natural causes". Third Schedule: unidentified; after custody and related to it; law enforcement operations; workplace accidents;
-- unlawful act or omission; unknown cause; suspicious circumstances (items 2 and 6 deleted by S 255/2023).
-- (2): otherwise the Coroner MAY decline if the death was natural and an inquiry unnecessary, or it is not in the public interest; (4)
-- reporting the reasons to the Public Prosecutor.
--
-- An execution is not a reportable death, yet an inquiry into it is mandatory. A death following medical treatment is reportable,
-- but no inquiry is mandatory. Neither is one into a suicide or a road accident.
GIVEN `the death` IS A Death, `the Coroner is satisfied it was natural` IS A BOOLEAN, `the Public Prosecutor requires it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `an inquiry is mandatory` IF
        `the Public Prosecutor requires it`
    OR  CONSIDER `the death`
        WHEN `a death in prison custody` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `a judicial execution` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `an unidentified body` THEN NOT `the Coroner is satisfied it was natural`
        WHEN `a death after release related to custody` THEN NOT `the Coroner is satisfied it was natural`
        WHEN `a death during a police operation` THEN NOT `the Coroner is satisfied it was natural`
        WHEN `a workplace accident` THEN NOT `the Coroner is satisfied it was natural`
        WHEN `a suspected homicide` THEN NOT `the Coroner is satisfied it was natural`
        WHEN `a death of unknown cause` THEN NOT `the Coroner is satisfied it was natural`
        OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `27 -- what an inquiry decides`

-- s 27(1): the inquiry is directed to (a) the identity of the deceased and (b) "how, when and where the deceased came by his or her
-- death". (2): the Coroner "is NOT to frame a finding in such a way as to determine any question of criminal, civil or disciplinary
-- liability" -- though not inhibited by the likelihood that liability will be inferred.
DECLARE Finding IS ONE OF
    `who the deceased was`
    `how, when and where the death happened`
    `a recommendation to prevent similar deaths`
    `that the employer was negligent`
    `that the driver is guilty of an offence`

GIVEN `the finding` IS A Finding
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Coroner may make the finding` IF
    CONSIDER `the finding`
    WHEN `that the employer was negligent` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `that the driver is guilty of an offence` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE
